Docker Hardening – Part 1: Non-Root Containers

Your current Flask application previously ran like this:

Flask Application
       ↓
Docker Container
       ↓
Kubernetes Pod

By default, many container images run processes as:

UID 0 = root

That is risky.

Why is root inside a container dangerous?

Suppose an attacker finds an RCE vulnerability in your Flask application:

Attacker
   ↓
Flask RCE
   ↓
Command execution inside container
   ↓
Who executes the command?
   ↓
ROOT ❌

If the container is misconfigured or there is a container escape vulnerability, root privileges can make the impact much worse.

The security principle is:

Even if the application is compromised, the attacker should have the minimum possible privileges.

So we will change:

ROOT
 ↓
Application

to:

Non-root user
     ↓
Application




Check your Flask app running in Kubernetes

Since your application is deployed in Kubernetes, first:
If the output is:

uid=0(root) gid=0(root)

❌ Running as root.

If:

uid=1000(appuser) gid=1000(appuser)

✅ Running as non-root.



Check which user the application process itself is using

This is even more important. Run:

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n dev -- ps aux

Look at the first column:

USER       PID
root         1

❌ Application process is running as root.

Or:

USER       PID
appuser      1

✅ Application process is running as a non-root user.

kubectl exec -it myapp-my-app-chart-56c7c9c4bd-5kmfl -n dev -- whoami

Security review of your Dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim

ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

WORKDIR /app

RUN useradd -m appuser

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

RUN chown -R appuser:appuser /app

USER appuser

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
The important security lines
RUN useradd -m appuser

Creates a dedicated application user.

Then:

RUN chown -R appuser:appuser /app

Gives that user ownership of your application files.

And most importantly:

USER appuser

This means:

Container starts
      ↓
PID 1 = python app.py
      ↓
Running as appuser
      ↓
NOT root





One important thing: Dockerfile alone is not enough

Even though the Dockerfile says:

USER appuser

Kubernetes can potentially override the image user through securityContext.

For example:

securityContext:
  runAsUser: 0

would force root.

So for proper Kubernetes container hardening, we should enforce it at both layers:

Dockerfile Security
        +
Kubernetes SecurityContext
        =
Defense in Depth



part 2 - Defense in Depth with Kubernetes SecurityContext

Our next practical step

Let's first inspect your current Helm deployment template and add the Kubernetes securityContext correctly.- check security context if  it exists

Run:

cat templates/deployment.yaml



Step 1 — Add this to values.yaml

Find the existing podSecurityContext and securityContext sections and set them to:

podSecurityContext:
  fsGroup: 1000

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL
What each control does
Control	             Protection
runAsNonRoot:             true	Kubernetes refuses to run the container as root
runAsUser:                 1000	Forces the appuser UID
allowPrivilegeEscalation:   false	Blocks privilege escalation
readOnlyRootFilesystem:     true	Prevents writing to the container filesystem
drop:                         ALL	Removes unnecessary Linux capabilities
fsGroup:                      1000	Helps ensure mounted volumes have appropriate group permissions


What drop: ALL means

Linux capabilities split some root privileges into smaller permissions, such as permissions related to networking, mounting, changing system settings, etc.

Your container configuration says:

Container process
       │
       ▼
Linux capabilities
       │
       ▼
DROP ALL ❌

So even if the process has a vulnerability, it has fewer kernel-level privileges available.



kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n dev -o yaml | grep -A10 "securityContext:"




Step 2: Read-Only Root Filesystem 🔒

Currently, if an attacker compromises your Flask application, they may be able to write files inside the container:

/app
/tmp
/root
/etc
...

Even though the container is non-root, preventing unnecessary writes reduces what an attacker can do.

We will configure:

readOnlyRootFilesystem: true

But first we need to understand something important.

Why this can break an application

With a read-only root filesystem:

Container filesystem
│
├── /app       🔒 Read-only
├── /etc       🔒 Read-only
├── /usr       🔒 Read-only
├── /root      🔒 Read-only
└── /tmp       🔒 Read-only ❌

Many applications need /tmp for temporary files.

Therefore, the secure approach is:

Read-only root filesystem
          │
          ▼
      Everything 🔒
          │
          └──── /tmp specifically writable
                       │
                    emptyDir
Step 2.1 — Add readOnlyRootFilesystem

First modify your securityContext in values.yaml:

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL

Don't deploy yet.

Step 2.2 — Add a writable /tmp volume

Your Deployment template already supports:

volumeMounts:

and:

volumes:

So again, we only need to configure values.yaml.

Add:

volumeMounts:
  - name: tmp
    mountPath: /tmp

volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}

Your relevant values.yaml sections should now look like:

podSecurityContext:
  fsGroup: 1000

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL

volumeMounts:
  - name: tmp
    mountPath: /tmp

volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}
Why emptyDir is useful

emptyDir is created when the Pod starts.

Pod starts
    │
    ▼
emptyDir created
    │
    ▼
Mounted at /tmp
    │
    ▼
Application can write temporary files

When the Pod is deleted:

Pod deleted
    │
    ▼
emptyDir deleted

So temporary attacker-created files don't persist in the container filesystem.

