Next: AppArmor

AppArmor — first principles

You now have Seccomp:

Application
     ↓
Allowed / blocked SYSCALLS
     ↓
Linux Kernel

AppArmor solves a different problem.

Imagine an attacker compromises your Flask application and gains code execution inside the container.

They may try:

Read /etc/shadow
Write to sensitive files
Access SSH keys
Access certain directories
Execute restricted programs

Traditional Linux permissions may not be enough as an additional defense layer.

AppArmor applies a mandatory policy to a process.

Conceptually:

Application Process
        │
        ▼
   AppArmor Profile
        │
        ├── Can access /app/**        ✅
        ├── Can write /tmp/**         ✅
        ├── Can access sensitive path ❌
        └── Other restricted actions  ❌

Kubernetes describes AppArmor as using program profiles to restrict individual programs, while Seccomp filters system calls.

Seccomp vs AppArmor

This distinction is extremely important:

Security Layer	Controls
Non-root	Who the process runs as
Capabilities	Which privileged kernel powers it has
allowPrivilegeEscalation	Whether it can gain additional privileges
Read-only filesystem	Where it can write
Seccomp	Which syscalls it can make
AppArmor	Which resources/actions the process is allowed to access

They provide defense in depth:

                    Flask Application
                           │
                           ▼
                     AppArmor
                 Resource restrictions
                           │
                           ▼
                     Seccomp
                  Syscall filtering
                           │
                           ▼
                     Linux Kernel



Add AppArmor to your Helm chart

We will add this at the container level, alongside your existing container hardening settings.

Your current securityContext in values.yaml should look approximately like:

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL

Change it to:

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL
  appArmorProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault






