1. Role vs ClusterRole — the critical distinction

You already created:

Role: pod-reader
Namespace: dev

That means the permission is limited to dev.

Think:

                 Kubernetes Cluster
        ┌────────────┬────────────┬────────────┐
        │            │            │            │
       dev          test         prod         ...
        │
        └── Role
            pod-reader

The Role says:

This permission exists within dev.

It does not mean:

get pods everywhere

It means:

get pods in dev



2. Let's prove namespace isolation

Your ServiceAccount:

system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo

has:

get pods → dev

Now ask:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n dev

Expected:

yes

Now:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n kube-system

Expected:

no

This demonstrates an important principle:

A RoleBinding does not magically grant the Role's permissions to other namespaces.

3. ClusterRole

Now let's create a ClusterRole.

nano rbac-clusterrole.yaml
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-cluster
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources:
      - pods
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch

Apply:

kubectl apply -f rbac-clusterrole.yaml

Notice something:

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-cluster

There is no namespace.

Why?

Because a ClusterRole is cluster-scoped.

4. ClusterRole doesn't automatically mean cluster-wide access

This is an important subtle point.

People often think:

ClusterRole = permissions everywhere

Not necessarily.

The binding determines how the permissions are applied.

For example, we can bind a ClusterRole to a ServiceAccount using a RoleBinding in dev.

ClusterRole
     │
     │
RoleBinding
     │
     ▼
ServiceAccount

In this configuration, the ClusterRole's permissions are used within the namespace of the RoleBinding.

So:

ClusterRole
    ↓
RoleBinding in dev
    ↓
ServiceAccount
    ↓
permissions in dev

This is a powerful RBAC design pattern because you can define one reusable ClusterRole and bind it separately in multiple namespaces.

5. Let's test that

Create:

nano rbac-clusterrole-binding.yaml
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-clusterrole-binding
  namespace: dev

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: rbac-demo
    namespace: dev

roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader-cluster
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Apply:

kubectl apply -f rbac-clusterrole-binding.yaml

Now:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n dev

Should be:

yes

The interesting part:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n kube-system

Should still be:

no

Because the binding is namespace-scoped to dev.

6. Now the dangerous one: ClusterRoleBinding

A ClusterRoleBinding applies the ClusterRole at cluster scope.

Conceptually:

ServiceAccount
       │
       ▼
ClusterRoleBinding
       │
       ▼
ClusterRole
       │
       ▼
cluster-wide permissions

For example, imagine:

kind: ClusterRoleBinding

binding:

rbac-demo

to:

pod-reader-cluster

Then the ServiceAccount could potentially:

get pods in dev
get pods in test
get pods in prod
get pods in kube-system
...

depending on the ClusterRole's resources/verbs.

That's why:

ClusterRoleBinding deserves much more scrutiny than a normal RoleBinding.

7. Let's demonstrate it

Create:

nano rbac-clusterrolebinding.yaml
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-cluster-binding

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: rbac-demo
    namespace: dev

roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader-cluster
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Apply:

kubectl apply -f rbac-clusterrolebinding.yaml

Now test:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n dev
yes

And:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n kube-system

Now:

yes

And:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n default

Also:

yes

That's the difference.

8. Visual comparison
Role + RoleBinding
                Role
                 │
                 ▼
           get/list pods
                 │
                 ▼
        ┌────────────────┐
        │ namespace: dev │
        └────────────────┘
ClusterRole + RoleBinding
             ClusterRole
                  │
                  ▼
             RoleBinding
                  │
                  ▼
        ┌────────────────┐
        │ namespace: dev │
        └────────────────┘

Reusable permission definition, namespace-limited binding.

ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding
             ClusterRole
                  │
                  ▼
        ClusterRoleBinding
                  │
                  ▼
       ┌────────────────────┐
       │ entire cluster     │
       └────────────────────┘

Potentially extremely powerful.

9. RBAC permissions are additive

This is another very important concept.

Suppose:

Role A
  get pods

and:

Role B
  delete pods

Both are bound to the same ServiceAccount.

The ServiceAccount gets:

get pods
delete pods

RBAC doesn't work like:

Role A says yes
Role B says no
→ deny

There is no conventional explicit-deny rule in Kubernetes RBAC.

Think:

Permission A
      +
Permission B
      +
Permission C
      =
Effective permissions

Therefore, when auditing a ServiceAccount, you need to examine all bindings, not just one Role.

10. Now let's talk about wildcards

This is where RBAC starts getting dangerous.

Consider:

verbs:
  - "*"

This potentially means:

get
list
watch
create
update
patch
delete
...

And:

resources:
  - "*"

means potentially:

pods
secrets
deployments
configmaps
services
...

Combining:

apiGroups:
  - "*"

resources:
  - "*"

verbs:
  - "*"

is effectively:

Give this identity extremely broad permissions.

🚨 In production, this should immediately trigger a security review.

11. Why secrets are especially dangerous

Imagine a compromised ServiceAccount has:

get secrets

An attacker may be able to retrieve Kubernetes Secrets accessible to that identity.

For example:

database password
API token
cloud credential
registry credential
application secret
TLS private key

So this:

resources:
  - secrets

verbs:
  - get

is much more sensitive than:

resources:
  - pods

verbs:
  - get

This is why a security engineer should treat:

secrets

as a high-value Kubernetes resource.

12. ServiceAccount compromise → RBAC compromise

Now we're getting into the security-engineering mindset.

Imagine your Flask application runs:

Pod
 ↓
ServiceAccount
 ↓
Kubernetes API

If an attacker compromises the application, they may gain access to credentials associated with the Pod's ServiceAccount.

Then:

Web vulnerability
       ↓
Application compromise
       ↓
Pod compromise
       ↓
ServiceAccount credentials
       ↓
Kubernetes API
       ↓
RBAC permissions
       ↓
Potential cluster impact

This is why Kubernetes RBAC is directly relevant to AppSec.

It's not just Kubernetes administration.

13. Example attack path

Imagine an application ServiceAccount has:

get secrets
list pods
create pods

An attacker compromises the application.

They discover:

ServiceAccount token

They query the Kubernetes API.

RBAC says:

get secrets → YES
create pods → YES

Now the compromise can potentially move beyond the original application.

The exact escalation path depends heavily on the other permissions, pod security controls, namespaces, mounted credentials, cloud integration, and cluster configuration.

This is why we don't simply ask:

"Does the ServiceAccount have cluster-admin?"

We ask:

"What can this identity actually do?"

14. kubectl auth can-i is your RBAC microscope

You already used:

kubectl auth can-i

Now learn the powerful form:

kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo \
  -n dev

This asks:

Show me the effective permissions of this identity in dev.

Run it.

You'll get something similar to:

Resources       Non-Resource URLs   Resource Names   Verbs
pods            []                  []               [get list watch]

Depending on the bindings you've created, you may see additional permissions.

This is one of the most useful commands for RBAC auditing.

15. Now inspect the actual bindings

Run:

kubectl get role -n dev
kubectl get rolebinding -n dev
kubectl get clusterrole
kubectl get clusterrolebinding

And inspect one:

kubectl describe rolebinding pod-reader-binding -n dev

You'll see the relationship:

Subject:
  ServiceAccount: rbac-demo

Role:
  pod-reader

That's the actual authorization chain.

16. Security mindset: follow the chain

When investigating Kubernetes RBAC, think:

IDENTITY
   ↓
SERVICEACCOUNT / USER / GROUP
   ↓
BINDINGS
   ↓
ROLE / CLUSTERROLE
   ↓
RULES
   ↓
API GROUP
   ↓
RESOURCE
   ↓
VERB
   ↓
EFFECTIVE ACCESS

For example:

system:serviceaccount:dev:rbac-demo
             ↓
       RoleBinding
             ↓
       pod-reader
             ↓
       core API group
             ↓
           pods
             ↓
       get/list/watch

If you can trace this chain, you understand Kubernetes RBAC.

17. One very important cleanup

We deliberately created:

ClusterRoleBinding

to demonstrate cluster-wide access.

Don't leave this unnecessarily attached to our demo ServiceAccount.

Remove it:

kubectl delete clusterrolebinding pod-reader-cluster-binding

Also, we don't need the extra ClusterRole for the moment:

kubectl delete clusterrole pod-reader-cluster

And we can remove the temporary ClusterRole/RoleBinding:

kubectl delete rolebinding pod-reader-clusterrole-binding -n dev

