rbac-demo
pod-reader
pod-reader-binding




3. Role = namespace-scoped

Your Role had:

kind: Role

metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: dev

The important line is:

namespace: dev

So this Role exists in dev.

Role
 └── namespace: dev

Its permissions apply to resources in that namespace when used through a normal RoleBinding.

4. ClusterRole = no namespace in metadata

Your ClusterRole:

kind: ClusterRole

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-cluster

Notice:

namespace: dev

is missing.

That's because ClusterRole itself is cluster-scoped.

Role:
  namespace: dev        ← namespace-scoped object

ClusterRole:
  no namespace          ← cluster-scoped object

But there's an important subtlety:

ClusterRole being cluster-scoped does not automatically mean its permissions are cluster-wide.

The binding determines how it's granted.

5. RoleBinding determines where the permission is granted

Look at:

kind: RoleBinding

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-binding
  namespace: dev

The:

namespace: dev

belongs to the RoleBinding.

Then:

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: rbac-demo
    namespace: dev

This identifies who receives the permission.

And:

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader

identifies which permission set they're receiving.

So:

RoleBinding
│
├── namespace: dev
│
├── subject:
│     dev/rbac-demo
│
└── roleRef:
      Role/pod-reader
6. ClusterRole + RoleBinding

This is the subtle one.

You had:

kind: RoleBinding

metadata:
  namespace: dev

but:

roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader-cluster

So:

ClusterRole
    │
    │ referenced by
    ▼
RoleBinding
    │
    │ namespace: dev
    ▼
ServiceAccount

The ClusterRole defines the permissions, while the RoleBinding limits where those permissions are granted.

So the same ClusterRole could be reused:

ClusterRole: pod-reader-cluster

        ┌──────────────┐
        │              │
        ▼              ▼
RoleBinding        RoleBinding
namespace: dev     namespace: test
        │              │
        ▼              ▼
ServiceAccount     ServiceAccount

This is one reason ClusterRoles are useful even when you don't want cluster-wide access.

7. ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide binding

Now compare that with:

kind: ClusterRoleBinding

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-cluster-binding

Notice there is no namespace here.

Then:

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: rbac-demo
    namespace: dev

The ServiceAccount is still namespaced (dev).

But:

roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader-cluster

is bound through a ClusterRoleBinding.

Conceptually:

dev/rbac-demo
      │
      ▼
ClusterRoleBinding
      │
      ▼
ClusterRole
      │
      ▼
cluster-wide permission scope

That's why our test changed from:

-n kube-system → no

to:

-n kube-system → yes
8. The easiest way to remember it

Don't think:

"Role vs ClusterRole determines everything."

Think about two separate questions:

Question 1 — Where is the permission definition?
Role
    → namespace-scoped

ClusterRole
    → cluster-scoped
Question 2 — How is that permission granted?
RoleBinding
    → namespace-scoped binding

ClusterRoleBinding
    → cluster-wide binding

So your four combinations are:

Permission	Binding	Result
Role	RoleBinding	Namespace-scoped
ClusterRole	RoleBinding	Permissions granted in that binding's namespace
ClusterRole	ClusterRoleBinding	Cluster-wide
Role	ClusterRoleBinding	❌ Not valid

The YAML is what tells Kubernetes all of this.

The lines I want you to start spotting immediately are:

kind: Role
namespace: dev

versus:

kind: ClusterRole

and:

kind: RoleBinding
namespace: dev

versus:

kind: ClusterRoleBinding

If you understand those four YAML structures, you've basically unlocked the core RBAC model.






🔥 RBAC Privilege Escalation

The key idea is:

A permission that looks harmless by itself can become dangerous when combined with another permission.



Why is Pod creation security-sensitive?

Because a Pod isn't merely:

"name: my-pod"

A Pod specification controls many things.

For example:

spec:
  containers:
    - image: ...
      securityContext:
        ...
      volumeMounts:
        ...

Depending on what the cluster permits, Pod configuration can influence:

filesystem access
Linux capabilities
host namespace access
mounted volumes
ServiceAccounts
network behavior
container privileges

So the security question isn't simply:

"Can this identity create Pods?"

It's:

"What kind of Pods can this identity create?"

That's a much better security question.





Three special RBAC verbs you must know

There are three permissions that should immediately get your attention during an RBAC review:

escalate
bind
impersonate

These are different from normal workload permissions.

9. escalate

Imagine someone has:

Role:
  get pods

They shouldn't simply be able to modify/create RBAC objects that grant themselves:

cluster-admin

Kubernetes has protections around modifying roles with permissions greater than the caller's own permissions.

The escalate permission is associated with bypassing those normal restrictions.

So when auditing RBAC:

escalate

should be treated as highly sensitive.

10. bind

bind relates to the ability to create or modify bindings to roles.

Conceptually:

RoleBinding
     ↓
Role / ClusterRole

Imagine an identity could freely bind itself to powerful roles.

That could become:

low privilege identity
        ↓
bind permission
        ↓
powerful Role
        ↓
high privilege

Therefore:

bind

is another permission that deserves serious scrutiny.

11. impersonate

This one is particularly interesting.

Imagine:

User A

is allowed to impersonate:

User B

Then requests can be evaluated as though they came from B.

Conceptually:

Attacker
   ↓
impersonate
   ↓
Powerful identity
   ↓
Powerful permissions

That's why:

impersonate

is another high-value permission during RBAC audits.

12. Your RBAC security checklist

When you see an RBAC rule, don't just read:

resources:
  - pods

Read the entire rule:

apiGroups:
resources:
verbs:
resourceNames:

Then ask:

WHO?
User?
Group?
ServiceAccount?
WHERE?
Which namespace?
Cluster-wide?
WHAT?
pods?
secrets?
deployments?
roles?
rolebindings?
WHAT CAN THEY DO?
get?
list?
create?
update?
patch?
delete?
bind?
escalate?
impersonate?





